The browser pane stops pretending and hands the page to Brave
==========================================================

The in-app browser fetched pages through `/api/quixi/browser/render` and put the result in a sandboxed
iframe. Whatever that is, it is not a browser: no real session, no logins, scripts neutered by the
sandbox, and it was wearing the chrome of one.

  * the address bar sends the page to Brave, which opens it as its own window (`--app=<url>`) rather
    than a tab inside whatever is already running
  * the frame is gone; in its place a short panel saying where pages go and why, with **Open in
    Brave** and **Copy address**
  * `/__eipc__/open-external` reports `app_window: true` when it launched a Chromium-family browser, so
    a caller can tell a real handoff from a fallback to `xdg-open`

This is the honest version of "use Brave". Making the WebView itself run Blink means replacing the
webview backend with CEF and shipping ~150 MB of Chromium — a different decision with a real bill
attached, not a detail to slip in.
